Title: "Functions"
Id: "40c5a61d-19e7-4856-8fb0-57d09ac6a6ed"


Text: "A function is the mathematical embodiment of a deterministic
       transformation, assigning to each input exactly one output. Historically
       conceived by Euler and Lagrange as symbolic algebraic formulas, the
       modern conception was revolutionized by Peter Gustav Lejeune Dirichlet
       and Richard Dedekind: a function $f : A \to B$ is an arbitrary rule of
       association, requiring neither analytic formula nor geometric continuity.

       In axiomatic set theory, a function is formally defined as a special kind
       of binary relation: a subset of the Cartesian product $A \times B$
       satisfying the dual requirements of existence (every domain element
       $x \in A$ has an image) and uniqueness (no domain element is assigned
       more than one image). Functions, and the binary operations that
       specialize them, constitute the dynamic machinery through which algebraic
       structures, topological spaces, and analytical dynamics are investigated."
Id: "37f9c062-435f-4310-b511-2d2d59281683"


SectionTitle: "Fundamental Function Types"
Id: "73c7927b-afab-4dde-b664-57f27c4b54db"


[\function:?on{A}:?to{B}]
Declares: f(x__) ::= y_
when: A, B is \set
specifies:
. x__ "in" A
. y_ "in" B
satisfies:
. forAll: x "in" A
  then:
  . existsUnique: y "in" B
    suchThat: f(x) = y
Documented:
. called: "function@[A]{ on $A?$}@[B]{ to $B?}$"
. written: "@[A, B]{f? \: : \: A? \rightarrow B?}"
. description: "A function $f : A \rightarrow B$ assigns to each element $x$ of
                the domain $A$ exactly one element $f(x)$ of the codomain $B$.
                The defining requirement is existence and uniqueness of the
                output: for every $x \in A$ there is one and only one $y \in B$
                with $f(x) = y$."
Id: "1e0cb28d-bc8f-42db-bcfa-86a215e88c7a"


Text: "Functions are classified by how their mapping action behaves across the
       domain and codomain:
       - An *injective* (one-to-one) function preserves distinctness: no two
         distinct inputs share a common output. An injection embeds the domain
         into the codomain without loss of information.
       - A *surjective* (onto) function covers the codomain entirely: every
         element in $B$ is realized as the output of at least one input in $A$.
       - A *bijective* function is simultaneously injective and surjective. A
         bijection establishes an exact one-to-one correspondence between the
         sets $A$ and $B$, demonstrating that they possess identical cardinal
         size and abstract set-theoretic structure."
Id: "252ce10f-e09f-4133-9110-e59fe497ce9c"


[\(injective)::function:?on{A}:?to{B}]
Refines: f(x__) ::= y_
satisfies:
. forAll: x1, x2 "in" A
  then:
  . if: f(x1) = f(x2)
    then: x1 = x2
Documented:
. adjective:
  . "injective"
  . "one-to-one"
. description: "An injective (one-to-one) function never sends two distinct
                domain elements to the same codomain element."
Id: "c33dd972-b742-4aed-8bbc-797ecdb2b5af"


[\(surjective)::function:?on{A}:?to{B}]
Refines: f(x__) ::= y_
satisfies:
. forAll: y "in" B
  then:
  . exists: x "in" A
    suchThat: f(x) = y
Documented:
. adjective:
  . "surjective"
  . "onto"
. description: "A surjective (onto) function hits every element of its codomain."
Id: "be68a628-8ca8-49c8-8780-bd7aa7401ef4"


[\(bijective)::function:?on{A}:?to{B}]
Refines: f
specifies: f is \(injective, surjective)::[[f]]
Documented:
. adjective: "bijective"
. description: "A bijective function is both injective and surjective,
                establishing a one-to-one correspondence between its domain and
                codomain."
Id: "554dad1c-22e4-47ae-9d4f-b0fb088de488"


SectionTitle: "Identity, Composition, and Inverses"
Id: "63269ec1-05b2-4389-8448-8596fd9f1c08"


Text: "On every set $A$, there exists a canonical trivial mapping: the *identity
       function* $\text{id}_A : A \to A$, defined by $\text{id}_A(x) = x$. The
       identity function acts as the two-sided neutral element for the algebra
       of function composition."
Id: "a698fee3-f3a3-4fc8-bb89-3d83f72b6bc9"


[\identity.function:on{A}]
Defines: f is \function:on{A}:to{A}
when: A is \set
expresses: f(x__) := x__
Documented:
. called: "identity function on $A?$"
. written: "\text{id}_{A?}"
. description: "The identity function $\text{id}_A$ maps every element of $A$ to
                itself: $\text{id}_A(x) = x$."
Id: "be488ff3-1570-44a8-8be0-2bc9cbcdd791"


Text: "When the codomain of a function $f : A \to B$ matches the domain of a
       function $g : B \to C$, they can be chained into a single compound
       mapping: the *composition* $g \circ f : A \to C$, defined by
       $(g \circ f)(x) = g(f(x))$.

       Function composition is universally associative:
       $(h \circ g) \circ f = h \circ (g \circ f)$. This associativity is the
       bedrock of category theory, where functions serve as the prototypical
       morphisms."
Id: "d6e88a37-b891-428d-bb2d-080654f19c81"


[g(y__) \.function.compose./ f(x__)]
Defines: h(x__) is \function:on{A}:to{C}
when:
. f is \function:on{A?}:to{B?}
. g is \function:on{B}:to{C?}
expresses: h(x__) := g(f(x__))
Documented:
. called: "composition of $g?$ and $f?$"
. written: "g? \circ f?"
. description: "The composition $g \circ f : A \rightarrow C$ applies $f$ first
                and then $g$, so $(g \circ f)(x) = g(f(x))$."
Id: "a5c4b516-227b-4893-aa18-5f04c1f005a4"


Text: "Every bijective function $f : A \to B$ admits a unique two-sided *inverse
       function* $f^{-1} : B \to A$ that completely reverses its action,
       satisfying $f^{-1} \circ f = \text{id}_A$ and
       $f \circ f^{-1} = \text{id}_B$."
Id: "5f0e98c0-5fb7-4b69-9811-55266a75deea"


[\inverse.function:of{f(x__)}]
Defines: g(y__) is \function:on{B}:to{A}
using: A, B is \set
when: f is \(bijective)::function:on{A}:to{B}
expresses:
. forAll: x "in" A
  then: g(f(x)) = x
. forAll: y "in" B
  then: f(g(y)) = y
Documented:
. called: "inverse function of $f?$"
. written: "f+?^{-1}"
. description: "The inverse function $f^{-1} : B \rightarrow A$ of a bijection
                $f$ reverses the mapping, sending each output back to its unique
                source input."
Id: "894fbbc8-5e3d-4fb0-b8c7-124bc834ebcf"


Text: "Beyond mapping individual points, a function induces actions on the power
       sets of its domain and codomain:
       - The *direct image* $f[X]$ pushes a subset $X \subseteq A$ forward into
         $B$, collecting all evaluations $f(x)$ for $x \in X$.
       - The *preimage* (or inverse image) $f^{-1}[Y]$ pulls a subset
         $Y \subseteq B$ back into $A$, collecting all inputs that map into $Y$.
         Crucially, the preimage $f^{-1}[Y]$ is well-defined for *any* function,
         requiring no invertibility of $f$."
Id: "14fa61b4-4f94-4156-aaf3-cefefc0af688"


[\function.image:of{f(x__)}:on{X}]
Defines: Y := \set@{f(x__) : x__ "in" X}
when:
. f is \function:on{A?}:to{B?}
. X \:subset:/ A
Documented:
. called: "image of $X?$ under $f?$"
. written: "f?[X?]"
. description: "The direct image $f[X]$ is the set of all outputs obtained by
                evaluating $f$ on elements of $X$."
Id: "70ff3a00-b34b-4e76-9bbe-5f3325a59f55"


[\function.preimage:of{f(x__)}:on{Y}]
Defines: X := \set@{x__ : x__ "in" A | f(x__) "in"? Y}
when:
. f is \function:on{A?}:to{B?}
. Y \:subset:/ B
Documented:
. called: "preimage of $Y?$ under $f?$"
. written: "f+?^{-1}[Y?]"
. description: "The preimage (inverse image) $f^{-1}[Y]$ is the set of domain
                elements mapped by $f$ into $Y$."
Id: "d2bd7f7c-06f3-4aff-a5e1-3399e5392f20"


SectionTitle: "Operations and Restrictions"
Id: "e2677e08-e77f-4228-8afc-5280b38b5241"


Text: "A *unary operation* (or endofunction) on $X$ is a function whose domain
       and codomain coincide: $u : X \to X$. Iterating unary operations
       generates discrete dynamical systems and discrete orbits."
Id: "4e45bf5d-1f34-4700-8d12-3a34c1eee921"


[\unary.operation:on{X}]
Declares: u(x__) is \function:on{X}:to{X}
when: X is \set
Documented:
. called: "unary operation on $X?$"
. description: "A unary operation on $X$ is an endofunction from $X$ to $X$."
Id: "3e224a1e-0519-4e11-b080-e748b38de02a"


Text: "A *binary operation* on $X$ is a function taking an ordered pair of
       elements from $X$ and returning a value in $X$: $* : X \times X \to X$.
       We write $x * y$ using infix notation. Binary operations are the
       foundational building blocks of abstract algebra, equipping sets with
       arithmetic structures such as groups, rings, and vector spaces."
Id: "dd492fce-0357-4b6a-810f-40764b5245f9"


[\binary.operation:on{X}]
Declares: x_ * y_ is \function:on{X \.set.cross./ X}:to{X}
when: X is \set
Documented:
. called: "binary operation on $X?$"
. description: "A binary operation on $X$ takes two elements of $X$ and returns
                an element of $X$. Formally it is a function from $X \times X$
                to $X$, written in infix form as $x * y$."
Id: "82c8948a-c30d-464b-8ab0-1c7bdb2ed770"


Text: "Two algebraic properties of binary operations recur constantly across
       higher mathematics:
       - *Associativity*: $(a * b) * c = a * (b * c)$, which ensures that
         bracket grouping is irrelevant, permitting unambiguous expressions such
         as $a * b * c$.
       - *Commutativity*: $x * y = y * x$, which ensures that input ordering is
         symmetric."
Id: "ded59881-7811-4fef-a95c-77f9801814e2"


[\(associative)::binary.operation:on{X}]
Refines: x_ * y_
when: X is \set
satisfies:
. forAll: a, b, c "in" X
  then: (a * b) * c = a * (b * c)
Documented:
. adjective: "associative"
Id: "e23d5d7d-eed8-4efc-bdda-d316af89684e"


[\(commutative)::binary.operation:on{X}]
Refines: x_ * y_
when: X is \set
satisfies:
. forAll: x, y "in" X
  then: x * y = y * x
Documented:
. adjective: "commutative"
Id: "fc0223f8-7053-44ae-a251-94b6c35bc31d"


Text: "Given a function $g : A \to B$ and a subset $X \subseteq A$, the
       *restriction* of $g$ to $X$, denoted $g|_X : X \to B$, is obtained by
       retaining the operational mapping rule while restricting its domain to
       $X$. Conversely, $g$ is said to be an *extension* of $g|_X$ to the larger
       domain $A$."
Id: "fc9215b0-5d36-4ee6-b3d6-e0007b8e39f5"


[\restriction:of{g(x__)}:on{X}]
Defines: f is \function:on{X}:to{B}
when:
. g is \function:on{A?}:to{B?}
. X \:subset:/ A
expresses: f(x__) := g(x__)
Documented:
. called: "restriction of $g?$ on $X?$"
. written: "g?\big |_{X?}"
. description: "The restriction $g|_X$ of a function $g : A \rightarrow B$ to a
                subset $X$ of $A$ is the function on $X$ that takes each input
                to the same value $g$ does. It keeps the rule and shrinks the
                domain."
Id: "ea7eab01-792c-427f-91dc-a75f43513b38"


SectionTitle: "Fibers and Factorization"
Id: "f8667ec9-767f-4c75-87a7-07d9ec2fb533"


[\fiber:of{f(x__)}:over{y}]
Defines: X := \set@{x_ : x_ "in" A | f(x_) = y}
when:
. f is \function:on{A?}:to{B?}
. y "in" B
Documented:
. called: "fiber of $f?$ over $y?$"
. written: "f+?^{-1}[\{y?\}]"
. description: "The fiber over $y$ is the set of all domain elements mapped to
                $y$."
Id: "e10a2ceb-8649-480a-9ab4-651ca4345f74"


TextTheorem: "Every function $f : A \to B$ admits a canonical *first isomorphism
              factorization*:
              $$A \twoheadrightarrow A / {\sim} \xrightarrow{\sim} f[A] \hookrightarrow B$$
              where $x \sim x'$ if and only if $f(x) = f(x')$.
              The three components decouple the structural actions of $f$:
              1. The canonical projection $\pi : A \twoheadrightarrow A / {\sim}$
              is a surjection identifying elements with identical values.
              2. The induced map $\bar{f} : A / {\sim} \xrightarrow{\sim} f[A]$,
              given by $\bar{f}([x]) = f(x)$, is a bijection.
              3. The canonical inclusion $\iota : f[A] \hookrightarrow B$ is an
              injection embedding the image into the ambient codomain."
Id: "cff9bfde-5ee9-4942-97a5-50b99637c887"


TextTheorem: "A function $f : A \to B$ with nonempty domain $A$ has a *left
              inverse* (retraction) $g : B \to A$ satisfying
              $g \circ f = \text{id}_A$ if and only if $f$ is injective. Dually,
              $f$ has a *right inverse* (section) $h : B \to A$ satisfying
              $f \circ h = \text{id}_B$ if and only if $f$ is surjective; the
              assertion that every surjection admits a right inverse is
              logically equivalent to the Axiom of Choice. A function possesses
              a two-sided inverse if and only if it is bijective, in which case
              the inverse is unique."
Id: "b0b5de59-0d45-45ea-ac3f-6abe4b3e8af1"


TextTheorem: "Direct images and preimages interact with Boolean operations
              according to fundamental preservation laws:
              - *Preimages preserve all Boolean structure*: for any family of
              subsets $B_i \subseteq B$,
              $$f^{-1}\left[\bigcup_{i \in I} B_i\right] = \bigcup_{i \in I} f^{-1}[B_i], \quad f^{-1}\left[\bigcap_{i \in I} B_i\right] = \bigcap_{i \in I} f^{-1}[B_i]$$
              and $f^{-1}[B \setminus Y] = A \setminus f^{-1}[Y]$.
              - *Direct images preserve unions*: $f\left[\bigcup_{i \in I} A_i\right] = \bigcup_{i \in I} f[A_i]$.
              - *Direct images preserve intersections only as inclusions in general*:
              $f\left[\bigcap_{i \in I} A_i\right] \subseteq \bigcap_{i \in I} f[A_i]$.
              Equality holds for all families if and only if $f$ is injective."
Id: "f143d5e8-5f5b-4208-a0b0-f8720288e434"
